The statement is **false**.

**Counterexample.** Let $X$ be an infinite set (e.g. $X = \mathbb{N}$) equipped with the **discrete topology**. Take as a basis for this topology the full power set $\mathcal{B} = \mathcal{P}(X)$, which is a valid basis (every open set is a union of elements of $\mathcal{B}$, and the basis-axioms are trivially satisfied since every subset is open).

Set $B = X$. Then $B \in \mathcal{B}$, so $B$ is a basic set. Let $A = X$, which is an infinite subset of $X$ with $A \subseteq B$.

**Claim.** $A$ has no limit point in $X$.

Recall that $x \in X$ is a limit point of $A$ if every open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ satisfies $U \cap (A \setminus \{x\}) \neq \varnothing$. In the discrete topology, the singleton $\{x\}$ is an open neighborhood of $x$. But
$$\{x\} \cap (A \setminus \{x\}) = \{x\} \cap (X \setminus \{x\}) = \varnothing.$$
Hence no point $x \in X$ is a limit point of $A$. Therefore $A$ has no limit point at all, even though $A$ is infinite and contained in the basic set $B = X$.

This contradicts the asserted conclusion, so the statement is false. $\blacksquare$

